{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Def6 uses K ⊆ F.theory, whereas WMT requires K = F_e: the machine must produce all and only the mind’s outputs. The missing reverse inclusion makes Def6 true for every K, since a recursively enumerable axiom set containing a contradiction proves every sentence."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Def5 correctly expresses equality with the theorems of the given axioms, but it does not require F to be effectively axiomatized. It can therefore hold even when F.theory is not recursively enumerable and cannot be the output set of any machine. Koellner’s formal systems have effective axiom presentations."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Def1 supplies a type of candidate sets, not a distinguished K or a criterion for absolute provability. Reading membership in a particular K as producibility by the idealized mind is an external interpretation. The type also fixes the language to closed first-order arithmetic, a restriction not stated in the quoted description of K."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Koellner’s F_e denotes a theorem set indexed by a natural number; Lean’s F denotes an axiom set, with F.theory denoting its theorems. Def6 quantifies directly over recursively enumerable axiom sets rather than their indices. This change of representation does not itself affect the existential claim."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Def5 gets theorem-set equality right when F is supplied as an effective system. Def6 does not express WMT: it asks whether one system covers K, not whether one system coincides with K."
}
